# docs.zd — the language documentation, read off disk.
#
# The same shape as `posts.zd` and for the same reasons: what reasons about
# a document lives here, what links to one lives with the routes in
# `site.zd`, because a `Card` links to a route variant and a route variant
# cannot cross a module boundary.
#
# What differs from a post is the order. A post is newest-first, which is a
# fact about its date; a doc is in the order somebody decided to teach it,
# which is a fact about the writing and has to be written down. That is the
# `order` field, and it is why `byOrder` sorts on a `Whole` where
# `newestFirst` sorts on a date's text.

use "./frontmatter" for fieldOf, bodyOf, slugOf, linkified

record Doc
    slug        is Text
    title       is Text
    # What the side navigation calls it, which is shorter than the title
    # more often than not: "How it works" against "How the language works".
    # A separate field rather than a truncation, because where to cut a
    # title is a judgement and the person who wrote it already made it.
    navTitle    is Text
    description is Text
    # Teaching order, ascending. Not the file name: `getting-started`
    # sorting before `language-tour` is a coincidence of spelling, and
    # `standard-library` before `examples` would be wrong.
    order       is Whole
    # `Markup` for `Prose`, exactly as a post's body is: `Text` would set
    # `nodeValue` and a heading would arrive as visible `#` characters.
    body        is Markup

# Written out, and the reason is `posts.zd`'s: a route parameter's `in`
# collection has to be a literal `static` list, because the compiler emits
# one document per value and the value set has to be settled before the
# build host has run anything. `build list` runs during the build, which is
# after. So adding a page is two edits — the file, and this line.
state docSlugs is static List of Text starting [
    "getting-started",
    "language-tour",
    "standard-library",
    "examples"]

state docs is static List of Doc from readDocs with directory is "content/zdeceptron"

function readDocs with directory
    from build list directory
    map each path to (docFrom of path)

# Read once and handed on, as `postFrom` does: there is no `let`, so "read
# it once" is spelled as a second function taking the text.
function docFrom of path
    give docFromRaw with path is path, raw is build read path

function docFromRaw with path, raw
    give Doc with slug is (slugOf of path),
        title is (fieldOf with raw is raw, key is "title"),
        navTitle is (fieldOf with raw is raw, key is "navTitle"),
        description is (fieldOf with raw is raw, key is "description"),
        order is (wholeFrom of (fieldOf with raw is raw, key is "order")),
        body is build markdown (linkified of (withoutTitle of (bodyOf of raw)))

# **The title, stated once.** These files were written for a renderer that
# showed the frontmatter title *and* the body, and every body opens with
# its own `# Getting started`. Rendered as they are, each page carries two
# h1s — which the outline check catches and calls what it is: a page is one
# document, and two h1s is two.
#
# The heading in the frontmatter wins, because it is the one the card, the
# sitemap and `llms.txt` already use, and a title that disagreed with them
# would be a fourth spelling of the same thing.
function withoutTitle of body
    if startsWith with value is (trim of body), prefix is "# "
        give after with value is (trim of body), delimiter is (newline)
    give body

# `order` arrives as the text of a number, because frontmatter is text. A
# page with no `order` sorts last rather than first: `0` would put an
# unmarked page ahead of the one somebody chose to open with.
function wholeFrom of text
    when parseWhole of text
        None
            give 999
        Some with n
            give n

state orderedDocs is static List of Doc from byOrder with all is docs

function byOrder with all
    from all
    sort each doc by doc.order

# The "no such page" page, built on the build host for `missingPost`'s
# reason: `build markdown` is readable only while the build runs, so a
# lookup that happens in the browser cannot construct one.
state missingDoc is static Doc from missingWith with title is "Not found"

function missingWith with title
    give Doc with slug is "",
        title is title,
        navTitle is title,
        description is "",
        order is 999,
        body is build markdown ""

function docWith with all, slug, index, fallback
    when listAt with value is all, index is index
        None
            give fallback
        Some with doc
            if doc.slug is slug
                give doc
            give docWith with all is all,
                slug is slug,
                index is index + 1,
                fallback is fallback
